4.0.0 (unreleased):
    * BC PHP 8.1 dropped, minimum version is now PHP 8.2
    * BC Symfony 6 dropped, add Symfony 8 support
    * BC Remove constructors from Interfaces (Liskov Substitution Principle violation)
    * BC Added QueryInterface::selectPrimary()
    * Fix collections and hydrators no longer Countable since 4.0.0-rc.1: count($collection) threw a TypeError
    * BC Remove the Services container, ContainerInterface and the pimple/pimple suggestion: wiring belongs to the integration (ting_bundle)
    * BC Replace the abandoned doctrine/cache with Symfony Cache Contracts: caches are Symfony\Contracts\Cache\CacheInterface pools, Ting\Cache\Cache only exposes get() and delete()
    * Fix UnitOfWork::detach() raising an "Undefined array key" warning when the entity was not queued
    * Fix changing a primary key: the UPDATE / DELETE targeted `WHERE key IN (old, new)` instead of the old value, and ignored a custom getter on primary keys
    * BC Fix Util\Debug, broken by typed properties since 4.0.0-rc.1: objects are now exported as arrays with a __CLASS__ key
    * Fix HydratorValueObject on cached queries (a cache hit returned raw rows): the hydrator now builds the objects itself, from the rows, with or without cache; ResultInterface::setObjectToFetch(), added in 4.0.0-rc.1, is removed
    * Fix cached prepared queries ignoring selectPrimary(true): they were always prepared on a replica
    * Fix UnitOfWork keeping a saved entity queued when it had no change: shouldBePersisted() stayed true and the entity was retained until detach/reset (leak in worker mode)
    * Fix Hydrator::objectSchemaIs() ignored with PostgreSQL since 4.0.0-rc.1: the schema read from the query, even empty, took precedence; objectSchemaIs() overrides it again, as in 3.x
    * Fix Mysqli prepared queries binding NULL for every parameter since 4.0.0-rc.1 (regression of ccmbenchmark/ting#108: parameter order lost, a repeated parameter bound once)
    * BC Fix a missing query parameter: every driver now raises a QueryException, prepared or not (Pgsql and Mysqli prepared queries sent NULL)
    * BC Fix the escaped colon (\:) kept in non-prepared Mysqli queries: it is now sent as ":", as by prepared queries and Pgsql
    * BC Remove Query\Cached\Query::setVersion(): it had no effect, the version is not part of the cache key since cache keys are manual
    * Ting\Cache\Cache used before setCache() now throws a ConfigException instead of an Error on an uninitialized property
    * Fix drivers used without ConnectionPool: a TypeError when no name (Mysqli, Pgsql) or no database (Mysqli) had been set; both default to an empty string
    * BC Remove the unused SerializerFactoryInterface argument from RepositoryFactory::__construct() (already removed from Repository::__construct() in 4.0.0-rc.1, now documented)
    * Fix connections without user or password (optional since 3.x) failing with a TypeError since 4.0.0-rc.1: DriverInterface::connect() accepts null; Pgsql leaves them out of the DSN so libpq uses its defaults
    * Fix Pgsql reads of a column without table (function, VALUES, CTE...) failing with a TypeError since 4.0.0-rc.1
    * Fix Pgsql Driver::execute() logging the rewritten SQL ($1) instead of the query with its named parameters since 4.0.0-rc.1
    * Fix Pgsql Driver::execute() never stopping the query log when the query fails
    * Fix Mysqli prepared queries without result set (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) returning false on success since 4.0.0-rc.1; they return true again, as Pgsql
    * Fix Serializer\BackedEnum::serialize() returning an int for an int-backed enum since 4.0.0-rc.1; it returns a string again, as in 3.x
    * Fix MetadataRepository registering an empty entity for a metadata without entity (PHP 8.5 deprecation "Using null as an array offset", '' listed by getAllEntities())
    * Fix Pgsql Statement destructor: no DEALLOCATE without connection, and no Error thrown from the destructor when the connection was closed (close(), reconnect())
    * Fix HydratorAggregator dropping the last group when the last row belonged to a group already yielded; the last group is keyed and built from its first row, like the others
    * Fix UnitOfWork updates and deletes sending the primary key of the WHERE clause without its serializer: an enum or DateTime key reached the driver as an object (error), a serializer whose output differs from __toString() matched no row; this includes primary keys whose serializer comes from their type (datetime, uuid, json...), which never got it
    * Fix the columns of the WHERE clause of get(), getBy() and getOneBy() not being escaped (reserved words such as "order" made invalid SQL; PostgreSQL lowercased mixed-case columns), unlike the SELECT and the UPDATE / DELETE clauses
    * Fix iterating or JSON-encoding a Collection without result (e.g. new Collection() returned as an empty collection): every hydrator raised a "foreach() argument must be of type array|object" warning
    * BC Fix UnitOfWork::pushSave() managing a new entity before its INSERT ran: saving it again after a failed INSERT (or pushing it twice before process()) ran nothing, without error, and left it without id; a new entity is now managed only once inserted (isManaged() is false until then)
    * Fix UnitOfWork::process() after a failed query: the failing entity stayed queued and the prepared statements stayed open, so every later save() / delete() on the shared UnitOfWork replayed the failed query first (silent re-INSERT, or the same error until reset()); the statements are now closed, the failing entity is unqueued (its changes are kept for a retry), the entities not processed yet stay queued, and the original exception is rethrown (not one from closing a statement that failed to prepare)
    * Fix UnitOfWork save of an entity whose setter notifies a property not mapped in the metadata: "Undefined array key" warnings then a TypeError; properties not mapped are now ignored (no UPDATE when nothing mapped changed)
    * BC Fix UnitOfWork losing the changes made in place to mutable values (a DateTime modified, a JSON object edited) without calling a setter: such a change is never notified, so the unit of work now keeps the database value of the mutable fields (new field option `mutable`, by default Serializer\DateTime, Serializer\Json without `assoc`, and serializers of your own) when an entity becomes managed (the raw value read, as the driver returned it, or the value serialized for an entity managed without being read, then the value last written), and writes a mutable field when its database value differs from it (and from it unserialized then serialized again) or its change was notified (when its value is known, see the fix of partial reads below): a value which does not survive a round trip (microseconds, a time in a DST gap, a zero date, a JSON big integer, JSON spaces) is not rewritten by an unrelated save, and a read serializes nothing, so it never fails because of the tracking (a field whose database value cannot be computed, a getter refusing null or a JSON value decoded to INF, is taken as unchanged unless set through its setter, so an unrelated save does not fail either); isPropertyChanged() follows; the other fields are still updated on notification only (same value as old and new value, objects included, is not a change). A mutable primary key targets its row by its kept database value, modified in place or not; a getter or a setter transforming the value read makes the next save write the transformed value
    * A "datetime" field without serializer hydrates a DateTimeImmutable when its property is typed DateTimeImmutable, nullable or not (same Y-m-d H:i:s format, immutable field); a property typed DateTimeInterface, DateTime or a union, or not typed, still gets a DateTime, as in 3.x (a DateTimeInterface property can hold a DateTime)
    * BC Fix NotifyProperty::__serialize() and __debugInfo() losing the private properties declared in a parent or child class of the class using the trait (silent data loss for entities stored in a cache or a session); both now return mangled keys (as get_mangled_object_vars()), entities serialized before are still read and payloads written by 4.0 are still read by 3.13
    * Fix Util\PropertyAccessor::setValue() throwing a ReflectionException, without explicit setter, for a property private in a parent class or only reachable through a setter (hydration, autoincrement id); it falls back to Symfony PropertyAccess. A PSR cache hit is now kept in memory: the pool was queried again on every write
    * Docs: UPGRADE-4.0.md lists the typed extension points (loggers, entity listeners, Repository::getCollection()); the 4.0.0-rc.1 entry on serialization interfaces is corrected
    * Security: prevent SQL injection in Pgsql Driver::getInsertedIdForSequence()
    * Include 3.13.0 changes: ResetInterface on ConnectionPool, UnitOfWork and Repository (worker mode)
    * Tests: migrate from atoum to PHPUnit
    * BC Rename master/slave to primary/replica: ConnectionPool(Interface)::primary()/replica(), Connection::primary()/replica(), QueryInterface::selectPrimary(), Repository::pingPrimary(), $forceMaster parameters renamed $forcePrimary, configuration keys "primary" and "replicas"; the old methods are removed and ConnectionPool::setConfig() throws a ConfigException on the old "master" / "slaves" keys (see UPGRADE-4.0.md)
    * BC Repository::get() composite keys and the $order of Repository::getBy() take property names instead of column names, like the criteria of getBy() / getOneBy(); a column name throws a ValueException naming the property to use (see UPGRADE-4.0.md)
    * BC Values of the criteria of getBy() / getOneBy() and of get() are converted with the serializer of the field: an object (enum, DateTime, Uuid...) is serialized, an array is an IN list of converted values, or a single serialized value for a serializer implementing the new marker interface Serializer\ArrayValueInterface (Json); a non-Stringable object without serializer throws a ValueException
    * BC An empty array, or null / a nested array inside an IN list, in the criteria throws a ValueException instead of sending invalid or never-matching SQL
    * BC Empty criteria in getBy() / getOneBy(), and get([]), throw a ValueException instead of sending invalid SQL ("WHERE " without condition): use getAll() to read every row
    * BC Add the marker interface Serializer\ScalarValueInterface, implemented by Serializer\Ip and the Boolean serializers of the drivers: a scalar criterion of getBy() / getOneBy() / get() (and each scalar of an IN list) for such a field is serialized, like save() does, instead of being sent as is (getBy(['ip' => '10.0.0.1']) compared an integer column with '10.0.0.1'; false was sent as '' to PostgreSQL); a scalar converted to NULL throws a ValueException
    * BC An order direction other than ASC / DESC in Repository::getBy() throws a ValueException instead of being silently ignored
    * Fix Generator::getByCriteria() emitting an empty " ORDER BY " when every direction of the order was ignored
    * Security: Mysqli Driver::execute() quotes string values with single quotes; double quotes allowed SQL injection under the sql_mode NO_BACKSLASH_ESCAPES (real_escape_string() then only doubles single quotes) and made values identifiers under ANSI_QUOTES
    * Fix Mysqli prepared queries running on the wrong database: one driver serves every database of a server, and a statement prepared while another database was selected was reused (reads and writes went to that database, its results reported it); statements are now cached per database, closeStatement() closes the statement of every database
    * Fix startTransaction(), commit() and rollback() ignoring failures (Mysqli and Pgsql): they throw a TransactionException with the driver error, including the PostgreSQL COMMIT of an aborted transaction, which answers ROLLBACK without error; a failed commit() or rollback() leaves no transaction open
    * Fix Mysqli ping() throwing an Error ("mysqli object is not fully initialized") forever once a reconnection had failed, even after the server came back: ping() retries the connection, and queries, prepares and transactions throw a QueryException / TransactionException until it succeeds; the database, charset and timezone set meanwhile are applied on reconnection
    * Fix HydratorRelational depending on the order of the relations (since 3.x): added in another order than parent to child, it duplicated or lost the roots, left relations unset and leaked joined aliases into the rows; the relations are now sorted topologically (a setter receives entities whose relations are already set), the rows grouped by the root alias, and every source alias removed from the rows, even when its join is empty
    * Fix HydratorRelational silently dropping a relation when two relations share the same source alias (since 3.x)
    * Fix Mysqli prepared queries without parameter failing with a ValueError (bind_param() called with no type)
    * Fix the Pgsql connection string built without quoting: an empty password made libpq read the next keyword ("port=...") as the password, values with spaces, quotes or backslashes broke it, and the connect error leaked the end of a password containing a space; every value is now quoted and escaped, and the user and password are fully redacted
    * Fix Pgsql getAffectedRows() after a prepared query (PreparedQuery::getAffectedRows()): it returned the count of the last non-prepared query; the driver now reports the last query, prepared or not
    * Fix Pgsql prepared statements reused after the connection was reset (ping() re-establishing a lost connection, reconnect(), close()): the cached statements no longer existed in the new session ("prepared statement ... does not exist"); they are now forgotten and prepared again, the old ones no longer DEALLOCATE a statement of the same name in the new session and throw a QueryException if executed, and closeStatement() still accepts their names
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser of Result::setQuery(): any asterisk was rejected (COUNT(*), 2 * id), only a column expanding to several columns is now (*, alias.*, schema.table.*, the fields of a composite or of a function returning a row: (p.loc).*, (p).*, (f(x)).*; parenthesized or aliased too), not a whole row inside an expression (alias.*::text, row_to_json(alias.*), o.* IS DISTINCT FROM n.*); the columns are split, and the FROM found, outside string literals ('...', E'...', $$...$$), quoted identifiers, comments (block comments nest, as in PostgreSQL: /* a /* b */ c */) and brackets ("'from' AS kw", "name || ', ' || id" or a comment with a comma broke the columns); the columns of a WITH query are read from its main SELECT, not from the first CTE
    * BC HydratorRelational throws a HydratorException when the relations form a cycle (bidirectional relation, alias related to itself); they were partially ignored
    * Fix a cached collection without result (e.g. a cached prepared query whose statement has no result set) throwing a TypeError on a cache hit since 4.0.0-rc.1: it comes back as an empty collection
    * Fix Hydrator::objectDatabaseIs() leaking to the other aliases (since 3.x): every table first read after the alias in a row was looked up in its database
    * Fix composite primary keys in the identity map of Hydrator and HydratorRelational (since 3.x): the values were joined with "-", so ("Jean-Pierre", "Paul") and ("Jean", "Pierre-Paul") shared an entity, and Hydrator looked entities up by one primary value only (a row could get another entity, a repeated composite key never got the same one); the key now holds every primary value, without collision
    * Fix Collection::first() and count() disturbing a foreach over the same collection (the result has a single cursor): first() inside the loop restarted it forever, count() on a cached collection ended it; first() returns the first item of the last iteration, CacheResult::getNumRows() counts without moving the cursor
    * BC Serializer\DateTimeImmutable (datetime_immutable fields) writes Y-m-d H:i:s by default instead of ATOM, and could not read a MySQL DATETIME / PostgreSQL timestamp without a format option; Serializer\DateTime and Serializer\DateTimeImmutable now read a value that does not match their format with the formats databases return (Y-m-d, Y-m-d H:i:s with optional fractional seconds and UTC offset such as a PostgreSQL timestamptz, ATOM / RFC 3339 values written by the old default) and reject anything else ("now", "+1 day", "05/06/2024"...); unSerializeUseFormat false still reads with the PHP date parser, and rejects an empty string instead of reading it as the current time
    * Fix Serializer\BackedEnum::unserialize() on an int-backed enum: a value that is not an integer ('abc', '') threw a TypeError instead of a Serializer\RuntimeException, and '1.5' returned the case 1 with a deprecation; only integers and integer strings are accepted
    * Fix Serializer\Json::serialize() throwing with the JSON_PARTIAL_OUTPUT_ON_ERROR option: the partial output is returned
    * Fix Serializer\DateTime and Serializer\DateTimeImmutable reading a format without time (e.g. Y-m-d for a DATE column) with the current time of day: the fields missing from the format are reset (midnight)
    * BC Fix setTimezone() (Mysqli and Pgsql) silently ignoring a timezone rejected by the server while recording it as current: it throws a DriverException with the database error and the timezone is not recorded. ConnectionPool applies the timezone database option on every primary() / replica(), so a rejected value, silently ignored by 3.x, now makes every query on that database fail: correct the value or remove the option (see UPGRADE-4.0.md); a timezone rejected when ping() / reconnect() applies it again is forgotten (the session keeps the server default) and the next setTimezone() reports it. Mysqli sends the timezone as a single-quoted, escaped string: double-quoted, it was read as an identifier under the sql_mode ANSI_QUOTES
    * Fix the charset re-applied by reconnect() (Mysqli) and ping() / reconnect() (Pgsql) staying recorded as current when the server rejected it (ignored set_charset() / pg_set_client_encoding() failure; a Pgsql reconnect() even returned false although connected): as for the timezone, it is forgotten (the session keeps the server default) and the next setCharset() reports it. Mysqli setCharset() throws a DriverException instead of a mysqli_sql_exception under MYSQLI_REPORT_STRICT
    * Fix Pgsql failed queries raising a PHP warning before Ting's exception: with an error handler converting warnings to exceptions (Symfony debug), an ErrorException escaped instead of the QueryException / TransactionException / DriverException, and the query log was never stopped; pg_query(), pg_query_params(), pg_prepare(), pg_execute() and pg_connect() are silenced, the query log is stopped whatever happens, prepare() without connection no longer starts it, the DEALLOCATE of a statement destructor no longer raises a warning, and setCharset() without connection throws a DriverException instead of an Error
    * Fix Mysqli prepare() leaving the query log started when the prepare fails (Pgsql stops it); the log of queries, prepares and statement executions is now stopped also when mysqli throws (report mode MYSQLI_REPORT_ERROR). Fix getInsertedId() and getAffectedRows() throwing an Error after a failed reconnection: they throw a QueryException, like the queries
    * BC Fix SphinxQL query parameters: null was sent as '' (with a "Passing null to parameter" deprecation) and false as ''; values are now quoted as with Mysqli, null as NULL and booleans as 0 / 1
    * Fix ConnectionPool sharing one driver between connections that differ: the key joined host, port, user and password with "|" (user "a|b" with password "c" collided with user "a" with password "b|c", a null user or password with an empty one) and ignored the driver class (a Mysqli and a SphinxQL connection to the same host, port and credentials got the driver of whichever was created first); Driver::getConnectionKey() now serializes the values (Pgsql still includes the database) and ConnectionPool keys the drivers by class too
    * Fix Util\Debug::export(): dates were exported without their value, unlike 3.x (now with their date and timezone, other internal objects with what var_dump() shows), a negative $maxDepth on a cycle recursed until memory ran out, the private properties of parent classes were missing, generators were consumed (no longer iterated) and a write-only virtual hooked property threw an Error (virtual properties are skipped)
    * Fix the INSERT of an entity whose typed property read by a custom getter is not initialized: the getter was called and threw an Error ("must not be accessed before initialization"); the field is skipped, as with a conventional getter
    * Fix Hydrator::mapObjectTo() with a null joined entity (LEFT JOIN without match) depending on the order of the columns (since 3.x): when the columns of $from came after those of $to, the method received an entity whose properties were all null and the row got the $from key back, set to null; the method is now never called for a null entity, and $from is always removed from the row
    * BC Fix UnitOfWork::manage() adding its property listener on every call (each delete then insert of the same entity added one more, and every change was then notified once per listener); the listener is added once per entity, and the changes of an entity not managed (detached, or new) are no longer recorded (a change made while detached is lost when the entity is managed again, see UPGRADE-4.0.md)
    * Fix UnitOfWork::pushDelete() of an entity never inserted (not managed and without primary key, or pushed with pushSave() before in the same process()) running "DELETE ... WHERE key IS NULL"; it is unqueued without query (an entity not managed but with its primary key is still deleted by that key)
    * Fix HydratorAggregator losing every row (no group, and a PHP 8.5 "Using null as an array offset" deprecation) when the callable given to callableIdIs() returns null; it throws a HydratorException, a group identifier is required
    * BC Fix the hydrator choosing the metadata of a table by registration order (since 3.x): when several metadata share a table on a connection (one per database or schema) and none matches the database and schema of the result, it took the first registered; it now takes the only one registered, else the only one of the same database, or when none shares the database the only one of the same schema, and otherwise throws a HydratorException naming the candidates (use Hydrator::objectDatabaseIs() / objectSchemaIs()). Schemas are compared case-insensitively when no exact match: Pgsql\Result lowercases the schemas read in the query, so a metadata with a mixed-case schema (mySchema) was never matched
    * BC Fix two repositories mapping the same table of the same database and schema (a full entity and a lighter projection) since 3.x: the last repository built hydrated the table for every read (UserRepository::get() returned a UserLight); the reads of a repository (get(), getBy(), getOneBy(), getAll(), getQuery()..., getCollection()) now hydrate the tables it maps with its own metadata, through the new Hydrator::preferRepository(), and a hydrator without preferred repository throws a HydratorException naming the candidates instead of taking the last one. The preference only chooses among the metadata of the database and the schema of the table (read from the result or named by objectDatabaseIs()/objectSchemaIs()), or among every metadata of the table when none matches them; a repository given to Hydrator::preferRepository() wins over the repository running the query; metadata hydrating the same entity with the same fields (a repository and its subclass) are not ambiguous
    * BC Fix two repositories mapping the same entity class (another table, a lighter projection) since 3.x: writes used the metadata of the last repository built (UserRepository::save() left out the columns UserNameRepository does not map, MainArtRepository::save() wrote into the archive table); Repository::save() and delete() now write with the metadata of the repository when they map the class of the entity (UnitOfWork::pushSave() and pushDelete() take them as a new optional parameter); pushSave() / pushDelete() called directly keep the metadata registered for the class of the entity
    * Fix a hydrator reused for another result keeping the metadata found for each alias of the previous one (another table or database under the same alias, another repository)
    * Fix PreparedQuery and Cached\PreparedQuery keeping the connection they were first prepared on: a query read on a replica then executed ran the write on the replica, and selectPrimary(true) after a first read kept reading the replica; the statement is now prepared again when the target connection (primary for execute() or selectPrimary(true), replica otherwise) changes
    * Fix Mysqli and Pgsql drivers keeping a transaction open across a reconnection: after a connection lost in a transaction and re-established by ping() or reconnect(), startTransaction() threw "Cannot start another transaction" and commit() "succeeded" on the new connection although the server had rolled back the writes; when the connection is replaced or closed (reconnect(), a failed Mysqli reconnection, a Pgsql ping() resetting the connection, close()), the transaction is now marked as lost: the next commit() throws a TransactionException, rollback() succeeds without sending anything, startTransaction() works again
    * BC Native types on every method of every interface and of every non-final class: every return value is typed, and every parameter and property but a few (the untyped, mixed-equivalent parameters of the built-in serializers and of the setResult() of the results, HydratorRelational::identityMap($enable), the callables of HydratorAggregator). An implementation or an override written for 3.x must declare the return types of 4.0, including every repository (MetadataInitializer::initMetadata() returns Metadata) and every overridden Repository method (getAll(bool $forcePrimary = false): CollectionInterface...). Among them: SerializeInterface::serialize() and UnserializeInterface::unserialize() take and return mixed (an implementation must declare a return type), CollectionFactoryInterface::get() returns Collection, CollectionInterface::first() returns mixed and setFromCache() takes a bool, Repository::get() / getOneBy() return ?object, Metadata::createEntity() returns object and getRepository() ?string, the remaining protected properties are typed (ConnectionPool, MetadataRepository, drivers, Query, Metadata, Repository), and the values cast before (Cached\Query::setTtl() / setForce(), Hydrator::objectDatabaseIs() / objectSchemaIs()...) are typed instead; the native extension handles (mysqli, PgSql) are typed object; the protected Pgsql Driver::setCollectionWithResult() takes the result as first argument and Pgsql Statement::setCollectionWithResult() requires its collection; see UPGRADE-4.0.md
    * Fix Mysqli Driver::execute() without collection throwing a TypeError since 4.0.0-rc.1 when the result set has no row; it returns null again, as in 3.x
    * Fix Query::query() throwing a TypeError since 4.0.0-rc.1 for a statement without result set on Mysqli (the driver returns true instead of the collection); it returns its collection, as PreparedQuery::query()
    * Fix Mysqli Driver::reconnect() called before connect() (since 3.x): it raised "Undefined array key" warnings then opened a connection with the default parameters of mysqli; it throws a NeverConnectedException, as ping()
    * PHPStan raised to level 8, without baseline, with a type coverage check (tomasvotruba/type-coverage) requiring native types
    * PHPDoc fully typed for static analysis (generics and array shapes, see UPGRADE-4.0.md): Repository<T>, CollectionInterface<T>, HydratorInterface<T>, QueryInterface<T>; PHPStan no longer ignores missing iterable value types and generics
    * Fix Mysqli and Pgsql drivers when the parameters of a query cannot be parsed (PCRE failure, e.g. pcre.backtrack_limit reached on a huge query): execute() and prepare() throw a QueryException instead of sending a null query on (deprecation, then an empty query or a TypeError)
    * Fix Mysqli Driver when mysqli_init() fails: the constructor throws a DriverException instead of leaving a driver without connection (an Error on the first call), reconnect() returns false instead of reconnecting the dropped connection
    * Fix Mysqli Result::getNumRows() and Pgsql Result without result (setResult() not called): no row, as the Mysqli iteration, instead of an Error or a TypeError; Pgsql Result::setQuery() before setResult() throws a QueryException
    * Fix Pgsql Statement::execute() before setConnection() and setCollectionWithResult() before setQuery(): they throw a QueryException instead of a TypeError
    * Fix the hydrators iterated without result (setResult() not called): they yield nothing, as count() gives 0, instead of raising a "foreach() argument must be of type array|object" warning
    * Fix Hydrator and its subclasses on a row of a result without connection name or database, and before setMetadataRepository() or setUnitOfWork() (set by the CollectionFactory): they throw a HydratorException instead of a TypeError or an Error
    * Fix Query::query() and its subclasses without collection on a query built without collection factory, and Cached\Query::query() / Cached\PreparedQuery::query() before setCache(): they throw a QueryException instead of an Error
    * Fix Metadata used before setConnectionName() / setDatabase() (getConnection(), so a Repository) or setTable() (the generated queries): it throws a ConfigException instead of a TypeError
    * Fix MetadataRepository::findMetadataForEntity() on an unknown entity without callbackNotFound: it does nothing, as findMetadataForRepository(), instead of throwing an Error
    * Add Connection::getName()
    * Fix Pgsql Driver::reconnect() before connect() connecting with libpq's default settings (empty connection string): it throws a NeverConnectedException, like Mysqli
    * Fix the parameter names of the generated queries (get(), getBy(), getOneBy(), INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) built from the raw column names (since 3.x): a column with a character outside [a-zA-Z0-9_-] (space, accent, dot...) made a placeholder the drivers could not resolve (missing parameter), an IN list on a column "a" (:a__1) collided with a column named "a__1", and an UPDATE lost the parameter of a numeric column name (array_merge() renumbered it). Parameters are now named <role><position>_<column> (v1_name for a written value, w1_id for a WHERE condition, w2_id__1 for an IN list), the column sanitized to [a-zA-Z0-9_]. Mysqli and Pgsql escapeField() double an embedded backtick / double quote
    * Add Driver\SequenceAwareDriverInterface (getInsertedIdForSequence()), implemented by the Pgsql driver: Metadata reads the id of an autoincrement field with a sequenceName through it, instead of checking method_exists()
    * Fix the save of an entity read partially (a SELECT of some columns, a join selecting some columns of the joined entity) overwriting the mutable fields it did not read with their PHP default (NULL, or a constructor default such as new \DateTime()): a mutable field is now written only when its value is known (read, set through its setter, or inserted)
    * Fix UnitOfWork::pushDelete() of an entity not managed but with its primary key marking it managed (without listener): after a failed DELETE (a foreign key, say), its changes were ignored and a save ran no query, and a pushSave() following the pushDelete() ran nothing; the entity stays not managed, so saving it updates its row by that key (see the save of an entity not managed below)
    * Fix a custom getter that handles its typed property not initialized itself (return $this->status ?? 'draft';) seen as unreadable since the fix of the INSERT of an uninitialized field read by a custom getter: the field was left out of the INSERT (and of the changes, the primary key checks and the mutable primary key values); the getter is now called, and only a "must not be accessed before initialization" Error makes the field unreadable
    * Fix an entity inserted a second time by its next save when writing back its generated id failed after a successful INSERT (a setter that throws, getInsertedId() failing): the entity stayed new and unqueued; it is now managed as soon as the INSERT has run, and the error is rethrown
    * Fix Pgsql Driver::reconnect() not reconnecting: pg_connect() returned the same link (same connection string, not closed), so the session, its open transaction and its prepared statements were kept while the driver forgot them ("prepared statement ... already exists", a later commit() committing the writes made before the reconnection), and a dead link was kept; reconnect() closes the link and every connection opens a new one (PGSQL_CONNECT_FORCE_NEW), so two Pgsql drivers with the same parameters no longer share a session either
    * Fix Pgsql ping() sending the charset and the timezone again on every call: in an aborted transaction the server refused them, they were forgotten, and every later access to the connection through the pool sent them again and threw before the ROLLBACK could be sent (connection stuck in the transaction); ping() applies them only when it re-established the session
    * Fix the Pgsql connection reopened by the pool after a failed reconnect() (or after close()) running with the server default charset and timezone: they stayed recorded as current, so the pool's setCharset() / setTimezone() sent nothing; setDatabase() now applies them to the session it opens
    * Fix a Pgsql prepared query failing in a transaction (since 3.x): the DEALLOCATE sent when its statement was closed (UnitOfWork::process()) was refused by the aborted transaction, the statement outlived the ROLLBACK, and retrying the transaction failed with "prepared statement ... already exists"; the driver deallocates such statements once the transaction ends (commit() or rollback())
    * BC Fix a PreparedQuery / Cached\PreparedQuery kept by the application unusable for good after the driver reconnected or reset its connection (Mysqli "MySQL server has gone away", Pgsql "prepared statement ... is no longer valid"): it prepared again only when the driver object changed; StatementInterface::isStale() (new) reports a statement whose session is gone, and the query prepares it again. Every statement of the session is marked stale, including one closed by name (closeStatement()) but still held
    * Fix Metadata::getRepository() returning null for metadata loaded by MetadataRepository::batchLoadMetadata() / batchLoadMetadataFromCache() without Metadata::setRepository() (since 3.x): it now returns the repository class they are registered under (a class giving metadata for hydration only, not a repository, still gives null)
    * Fix HydratorAggregator with a group identifier neither int nor string: a Uuid object or an array (composite identifier) threw a TypeError, a float was truncated with a deprecation; such identifiers are compared by their serialized form, and one which cannot be serialized throws a HydratorException
    * Fix HydratorRelational collapsing the whole result into one row when an alias of a relation was not in the result (a typo in AggregateFrom / AggregateTo): it throws a HydratorException; the rows without root entity (LEFT JOIN on the root side), which collapsed into one row holding a null root, are skipped
    * Fix HydratorValueObject overwriting a static property of the class with a column of the same name: the column is handled as one without property (a dynamic property)
    * Fix Serializer\BackedEnum accepting an int value followed by a newline ("1\n" gave the case 1): the value must hold an integer and nothing else
    * Fix Serializer\Ip::unserialize() on a value which is not an integer: "abc" or "" threw a TypeError, "1.5" gave 0.0.0.1 with a deprecation, and a value beyond 32 bits wrapped; it throws a Serializer\RuntimeException unless the value is an integer from -2147483648 to 4294967295 (signed or unsigned 32 bits column)
    * Fix Serializer\Json ignoring the JSON_OBJECT_AS_ARRAY flag (assoc defaulted to false instead of null, as json_decode()) and, with JSON_THROW_ON_ERROR, letting a JsonException escape, or failing a valid value on the error left by a previous json_*() call: errors always throw a Serializer\RuntimeException; a json field decoded to arrays by JSON_OBJECT_AS_ARRAY is immutable by default
    * Fix Util\Debug::export() throwing a TypeError on an iterable with object keys (a WeakMap: the UnitOfWork, a repository holding it, an entity with a WeakMap property): such an iterable is exported as a list of ['key' => ..., 'value' => ...] pairs; an iterator over a generator (IteratorIterator...) or a NoRewindIterator is no longer consumed
    * Fix cached queries accepting a negative TTL, with which the result was never cached: setTtl() throws a QueryException; its docblock now says that 0 applies the default lifetime of the pool
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) on SELECT DISTINCT / ALL / DISTINCT ON (...) (the first column read as "DISTINCT", without table, so not hydrated), ARRAY[a, b] (split at its comma, the next values shifted), a quoted alias with a space (AS "the id", truncated) and IS [NOT] DISTINCT FROM (taken for the FROM clause, the next columns lost)
    * Fix the INSERT of an entity without any value (only an autoincrement field, or no field initialized) with PostgreSQL (since 3.x): INSERT INTO t () VALUES () is MySQL syntax; Add Driver\DefaultValuesInsertDriverInterface, implemented by the Pgsql driver, which gives INSERT INTO t DEFAULT VALUES
    * Fix a column name holding a colon after a non-word character ("x :y") breaking every generated query on Mysqli and Pgsql (since 3.x): its :y was taken for a placeholder; Mysqli and Pgsql escapeField() escape the colon (\:), which the drivers unescape as in any SQL they are given
    * Fix NotifyProperty ignoring the __sleep() of an entity (since 3.12, when __serialize() was added): the properties it leaves out were serialized anyway (into the cache, for instance); __serialize() now keeps only the properties __sleep() lists, as serialize() does, and never the listeners
    * BC Fix saving an entity not managed with its id (a clone, a detached entity, one read from a cache or a session, one built with its id) inserting a new row (its autoincrement id left out of the INSERT, then overwritten): when its metadata have an autoincrement field and that property holds a value other than null (read from the property, not through its getter), it is updated by its primary key with every readable mapped field, then managed (isNew() is false for it); with that property null or not initialized, or without autoincrement field, it is inserted as before. An id defaulting to 0 is an id (declare it nullable), a deleted entity keeps its id (saving it again updates no row), and an entity read partially then detached or cloned, or restored from a cache with properties left out by __sleep(), writes its default values over the columns it did not read; an autoincrement field without property of its name (getter / setter options) is always inserted. To duplicate a row, set the autoincrement property of the clone to null (the sample entities no longer cast a null id to 0). addPropertyListener() no longer adds a listener twice (a clone keeps the listeners of its original)
    * Fix the retry of a transaction rolled back: an entity inserted in it, which keeps its generated id, is inserted again by its next save instead of updating a row that no longer exists. The Mysqli and Pgsql drivers implement the new optional Driver\RollbackListenerAwareDriverInterface: the unit of work registers each entity it inserts in a transaction, and the driver tells it when the transaction ends without being committed (rollback(), failed COMMIT or PostgreSQL COMMIT answering ROLLBACK, connection lost or closed); the unit of work then stops managing the entity, without modifying it, until its next INSERT (queued for a save, it keeps its place in the queue, for its INSERT); the marks survive detach(), detachAll() and reset(). The entities updated in the transaction stay managed and, pushed again as they are, write nothing (read them again, or detachAll() first). A COMMIT failing because the connection was lost (MySQL server gone away or lost connection, broken PostgreSQL connection) has an unknown outcome, the server may have committed, but is treated as a rollback: the retry inserts the entities again, which duplicates their rows if the server had committed, so check the rows (or use a natural / unique key) before retrying after such a failure. With a driver not implementing the interface, the entity stays managed as if its row existed (saved again, it writes nothing, or its changes by an UPDATE matching no row): detach it and set its id back to null
    * BC Fix two repositories mapping the same class (a full entity and a lighter projection) sharing the metadata registered last to track an entity: the unit of work keeps the metadata each entity was read with (UnitOfWork::manage() takes them as an optional second argument, given by the hydrators) and computes with them the fields not read and the kept values of the mutable fields; saved through other metadata, the fields the reading metadata do not map are written only once known (set through their setter, or written by an INSERT or an UPDATE), and a save forgets only the changes it wrote (and those no metadata of the class maps); an override of manage() or of the protected Metadata::getPrimariesKeyValuesByProperties() must declare and pass on their new optional parameter. An entity read through the projection and saved through the full repository no longer overwrites the fields it did not read, an entity read in full is no longer rewritten when the projection is registered last, and a save through the projection no longer loses the changes it does not write
    * BC Fix a hydrator with identity map given several results (kept as a service, worker mode) returning the entities of an earlier result, of another table under the same alias, or stale and no longer managed after UnitOfWork::reset() or detach() (saved, they were inserted again): the identity map lives for one result (a result read while another is iterated keeps its own), and an entity it returns again is managed again if it was detached, the fields its row did not read still left out of its saves; the protected Hydrator::$alreadyManaged property is removed
    * Fix SphinxQL Driver::escapeField() called without field (the default null of the interface) throwing a TypeError: it returns the field as a string
    * BC Fix PostgreSQL booleans on a `bool` field without serializer (since 3.x): 'f' was hydrated as true, and a PHP false sent by the Pgsql driver as '' (pg_query_params / pg_execute), which PostgreSQL rejects for a boolean; 'f' is read as false, and the Pgsql driver sends a PHP boolean as '1' / '0' (a false bound to a text column now stores '0' instead of ''); see UPGRADE-4.0.md
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) on quoted identifiers holding another character than [a-z0-9_$] (a colon, a space, an accent, a dash, ""): such a column, table or schema, even in the SQL Ting generates (getAll(), getBy()...), was not hydrated; quoted identifiers are unescaped ("a""b" is a"b), aliases included
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser on a query made of parenthesized SELECTs, as (SELECT ...) UNION ALL (SELECT ...): it threw "can't parse columns" (3.x read the whole query as a single column, not hydrated); the columns are those of the first SELECT
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser on "a IS [NOT] DISTINCT FROM b" without alias: its right operand was taken for an alias (column named "b"); it is named ?column?, as PostgreSQL names it
    * Fix NotifyProperty::__serialize() resolving silently the bare name, returned by __sleep(), of a private property of a parent class (the class using the trait sees it): as serialize(), it is left out with the warning "does not exist" (an E_USER_WARNING, PHP raises an E_WARNING); bare names are resolved against the class of the entity and the non-private properties of its parents
    * Fix Cache::get() logging a read which fails before its callback (a reserved character in the key, a failing pool) as a hit: it is logged as a miss, as CacheLoggerInterface only knows hits and misses; Cache::delete() stops its log when the pool throws
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) on a statement ending with ";" right after the alias of its last table (FROM app.users u;): the alias and the schema of that table were lost, so its columns without table prefix were attributed to the table name instead of its alias and one row was hydrated into two entities
    * BC Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) on a query of several statements, which pg_query() runs (a query without parameters) returning the result of the last one: the columns were read from the first statement, or from both (SELECT id FROM t; SELECT name FROM u was read as "id", it is now keyed "name"; SELECT set_config('x', 'y', true); SELECT u.id, u.name FROM users u lost the table of u.id); they are read from the last statement, so a query whose last statement returns no column (BEGIN; SELECT ...; COMMIT) throws "can't parse columns" as that statement alone does once every statement has run (3.x did not throw: it read the columns of the first SELECT). A ";" followed only by blanks or comments ends its statement: SELECT nextval('s') AS id;\n read the ";" into its last column, named "?column?" instead of its alias
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) on a dollar-quoted string whose tag holds non-ASCII letters, as PostgreSQL allows ($é$, FROM x$é$): it was not read as a string literal, so its commas and its FROM split the select list
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) taking a time quadratic in the length of a run of blanks or comments inside a column of the select list (13 s for 80,000 comments between two operands): the alias of a column is found in linear time
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) taking a time quadratic in the length of a run of double quotes inside a column of the select list, in a quoted identifier or a string literal (12 s for f("""...""") holding 128,000 doubled double quotes, 3 s for f('"""...') holding 128,000 double quotes): such a run is read in linear time
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) on a -- comment ended by a carriage return alone (\r, old Mac line endings): the comment ran until the next line feed, swallowing the columns and tables after it (SELECT p.id -- key\r, p.name FROM people p lost name); PostgreSQL ends it at a line feed or a carriage return
    * BC Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) hydrating shifted values when the columns it reads from the query are not as many as the columns of the result (a misread query, INSERT ... SELECT a, b ... RETURNING id, EXPLAIN SELECT a, b ...): each column took the value of another one, or null; setQuery() now throws a QueryException naming both numbers. A result without column (SELECT ... INTO, SELECT FROM t) gives rows without column, or an empty collection, where reading the table of its parsed columns failed (pg_field_table() has no field to read: a ValueError on PHP 8)
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) losing tables silently: its table regex exhausted the PCRE backtrack limit on an identifier of about 28 characters or more followed by another character than a space (FROM customer_order_line_items_archive_2024), JOIN some_long_function_name(1)...), the failure was not checked and the tables after it were lost; the unescaped "." between schema and table gave the first table of FROM users,orders o as the schema of orders, and only the first item of a FROM list was read. The tables are now read from the tokens of the query: every item of a FROM list (after the ON condition of a join too: JOIN b ON ..., c; inside a parenthesized join, which is no sub-query: FROM (a JOIN b ON ...), FROM ((SELECT ...) s JOIN b ON ...)), a one-character table (FROM t), ONLY / LATERAL, and no table for a function, a sub-query or the FROM of extract(year FROM x); a table or an alias read again in a sub-query (IN (SELECT ...), EXISTS, a LATERAL join, a CTE) no longer replaces the one of the main SELECT (since 3.x, the last one read won: the row was hydrated with the schema or the alias of the sub-query), and a table read only in a derived table (FROM (SELECT ... FROM people p) s) or in a CTE (WITH c AS (SELECT ... FROM people p)) is keyed by its name, as in 3.x, the alias of the sub-query being invisible outside of it (a LATERAL sub-query keeps it, as in 3.x); every PCRE failure of the parser throws a QueryException
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser on a UNION whose first SELECT alone is parenthesized ((SELECT ... ORDER BY ... LIMIT 1) UNION ALL SELECT ...): the columns were named after the second SELECT; a query starting with parentheses around a SELECT takes its columns from that SELECT, as PostgreSQL does
    * BC Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) on an expression without alias ending in an identifier (NOT flag, -amount, price - qty, a IS DISTINCT FROM t.b, id::text, x::double precision, ts AT TIME ZONE tz): its last identifier was taken for an alias (NOT flag named "flag", the expression cut to "NOT"; x::double precision named "precision"); an identifier is an alias without AS only after an operand (a name, a literal, a parenthesis), and not after the END of a nested CASE nor in count(*) OVER w, x::double precision or interval '1' day. An expression without alias is now named by a minimal rule (3.x named it after its last identifier or its whole text): a cast of a column after the column (id::text, CAST(u.id AS text) "id", a column of schema.table.column "column"), a function call cast with :: or not after the function (count(id) "count", now()::date "now"), a keyword without parentheses as a column (CURRENT_TIMESTAMP "CURRENT_TIMESTAMP"), anything else "?column?" (price * qty, 'x'::text, CASE ... END, ts AT TIME ZONE 'UTC', u.tags[1], (x)::text, (f(x)), CAST(count(*) AS int), a call followed by OVER, FILTER or WITHIN GROUP (count(*) OVER w), a scalar sub-query, TRUE, NULL), whatever PostgreSQL names it ("text", "case", "timezone", "tags", "count"...). Names keep the spelling of the query, as column names do (COUNT(id) "COUNT", TRIM(x) "TRIM" where PostgreSQL says "btrim"); unquoted names of columns, tables and aliases may hold non-ASCII letters, as in PostgreSQL (SELECT ü.prénom AS clé FROM usérs ü: 3.x split them, the column was hydrated in no entity), and the select list of a SELECT without FROM ends at its clause (SELECT 1 AS x LIMIT 1 is named "x", not "1 AS x LIMIT 1"). The keys of such unaliased expressions change for HydratorArray, HydratorValueObject and the "0" object (NOT flag was "flag", -amount was "amount", 'x'::text was "text": they are "?column?"; x::double precision was "precision", it is "x"; now()::date was "date", it is "now"; count(id) was "count(id)", it is "count"): alias every computed expression with AS
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser (since 3.x) on mixed-case identifiers: it lowercased every table, schema and pg_field_table() name and kept the case of the columns, so a table created quoted ("Users") never found metadata declared setTable('Users'), and SELECT u.ID did not find the metadata column id. Identifiers are now resolved as PostgreSQL does (quoted ones keep their case, unquoted ones are folded to lowercase, pg_field_table() names are kept as is), and the hydrator matches tables (MetadataRepository::findMetadataForTable()) and columns (Metadata::findColumns()) exactly first, then case-insensitively: the names 3.x matched are still matched (a "Users" table with setTable('users'), a column read as id with columnName ID), so a 3.x application needs no change. The metadata of the exact table name win, chosen from by the usual rules, unless a schema read contradicts them (none is without schema or of that schema) and a case variant has metadata of that schema and of the database read, which then win: neither the database read alone (for MySQL, the current database, a guess) nor a preferred repository chooses a case variant over the exact name; without metadata of the exact name, the metadata of every case variant are chosen from by the usual rules (schema and database read, preferred repository, database, schema, same entity), and a HydratorException names them when they cannot choose; several columns of the metadata differing only by case from the column read, none with its exact name, throw a HydratorException too, and a column is matched case-insensitively only when the row holds no column of its alias under the exact name (a table with the columns "Name" and name, only "Name" mapped: name does not overwrite its field). The aliases, keys of the row, stay in lowercase as in 3.x (FROM "MyUsers" "U" gives u); two aliases differing only by case whose columns are read ("U" and u) throw a QueryException instead of mixing their columns into one entity, unless one of them is the name of a table read only in a derived table or a CTE (FROM users u JOIN (SELECT name AS nm FROM "U") s), which the query cannot rename: its columns are then keyed by no table, as 3.x did. The name of a column (HydratorArray, HydratorValueObject, the "0" object) keeps the spelling of the query. The fallback applies to MySQL results too: a column read as BOO_NAME now matches the metadata column boo_name (MySQL column names are case-insensitive), and a table T_BOUH the metadata of t_bouh when no metadata has its exact name (two distinct tables only under lower_case_table_names=0)
    * Fix MetadataRepository taking two metadata of a table for interchangeable when their fields differ only loosely (the JSON option assoc null and false, 1 and true, "1" and 1): the fields were compared with !=, so the hydrator picked either of two repositories that hydrate differently instead of throwing the HydratorException of an ambiguous table; fields are now compared strictly, whatever the order of their keys, objects by class and properties, closures by identity, two NAN as the same value
    * Fix the Pgsql column parser on a query holding a large comment, string literal ('...', E'...', $$...$$) or quoted identifier (about 1 MB or more, less without PCRE JIT): its patterns exhausted the PCRE backtrack limit, and the query failed (silently before 4.0, with a QueryException since); they no longer backtrack on such a body. The backtrack limit still bounds the size of a body, depending on its content and on PCRE JIT (without JIT, about 1 MB of a literal of doubled quotes '', of a quoted identifier of doubled quotes "" (a column or an alias), or of a comment of "a*b " repeated; less, from about 250 KB, for a comment holding many nested /* */ pairs), and is reported by a QueryException
    * Fix a save or a delete of a managed entity serializing the old value of every field changed through its setter, though only the old value of a primary key is written (it targets the row): a serializer refusing that old value (a PHP default not read by a partial SELECT, '' for Ip, then setIp('10.0.0.1')) made the save and the delete throw; the old value of another field is no longer serialized, and a DELETE no longer serializes the fields that are not primary keys
    * Docs: the retry of a batch after a failed process() is corrected (docs/unit-of-work.md, Retrying a failed batch): the entities after the failed query stay queued, and an entity still queued keeps its place when pushed again, so pushing the batch again as it is wrote those entities before the ones pushed ahead of them (a child before its parent); call detachAll(), then read again the entities of the batch whose row existed before the batch, read from the database or built by hand and saved earlier (a detached entity is inserted when it holds no autoincrement id, or overwrites the columns a partial read left out), then push the batch again in its order; outside a transaction, leave out a failing entity that isManaged() reports managed before detachAll() (its INSERT ran, setting its generated key failed). The docs no longer say that a detached entity with its id is saved by an UPDATE of every field: only one whose autoincrement property holds its id, one whose key has no autoincrement column (a natural key) is inserted (duplicate key)
    * Docs: the entities read during a transaction that does not commit stay managed with the values read; only the objects inserted by the unit of work are told of the rollback, so an object read again for a row inserted in the transaction keeps its id and its save updates no row, silently: read such entities again after the rollback
    * Docs: the 4.0 section of UPDATE.md, left from 4.0.0-rc.1 and contradicting the code (PHP 8.1, PHPStan level 7, DriverInterface::setTimezone()...), is replaced by a pointer to UPGRADE-4.0.md and to this CHANGELOG
    * Fix a read nested in a setter running during the hydration of a row (a lazy lookup) with the same hydrator (a regression of 4.0): it replaced the state of the row being hydrated, which failed with an Error ("Undefined array key", then getPropertiesNotRead() on null), and with HydratorRelational lost the relations collected so far; the state of the row is now given back once the nested result is read, each result keeping its own identity map (3.x gave the rest of the outer result the entities of the nested one under the same alias)
    * Fix the case-insensitive match of a column when the metadata map several columns differing only by case ("Name" and name) and the row holds each of them under its exact name: a third variant ("NAME") threw a HydratorException; it now goes to the "0" object, and matches the only column of the metadata the row does not hold under its exact name otherwise (a row of "Name" and "NAME" hydrates name from "NAME")
    * Fix several columns of a row matching the same column of the metadata case-insensitively ("NAME" and "Name" read, name mapped, no column name read): the last one silently overwrote the field; they now throw a HydratorException naming them
    * Fix a hydrator extending Hydrator whose hydrateColumns() reads a result with the same hydrator once parent::hydrateColumns() has returned (a regression of 4.0.0, since the fix of a read nested in a setter above): that read replaced the state of the result being iterated, so its next rows were hydrated with the metadata and the identity map of the nested result (entities of the wrong class, null, or entities of the nested result); the hydration of a row now lasts until hydrateColumns() returns
    * Fix Cache::get() logging the computation of a miss inside its read: a callback calling get() on the same cache logged start, start, stop, stop, which a logger stopping the last operation started (ting_bundle's CacheLogger) misread, and the duration of a miss included the computation (the query of a cached query); a miss is now stopped before its callback is called, so the read measures the lookup only and the operations of the callback come after it
    * BC Fix several columns of a row matching the same column of the metadata case-insensitively ("NAME" and "Name" read, name mapped, no column name read): the last one silently overwrote the field (3.x matched neither and put both in the "0" object); they now throw a HydratorException naming them

4.0.0-rc.1 (2025-12-15):
    * BC PHP 8.0 dropped, minimum version is now PHP 8.1
    * BC All classes now fully typehinted with strict types and return types
    * BC Pgsql Driver now uses native PHP 8.1+ PgSql\Connection and PgSql\Result classes instead of resources
    * Serializers typehinted (SerializeInterface and UnserializeInterface keep their 3.x signatures: the native return types were removed before this release)
    * BC Added ping() and setTimezone() methods to DriverInterface
    * BC Added ConnectionPoolInterface::setDatabaseOptions() method
    * BC Deleted Metadata::getGetter() and Metadata::getSetter() methods
    * BC Deleted deprecated getInsertId() methods on Mysqli and Pgsql drivers (use getInsertedId() instead)
    * BC Deleted deprecated getInsertIdForSequence() on Pgsql driver (use getInsertedIdForSequence() instead)
    * BC Deleted deprecated getInsertId() on QueryInterface and Query class (use getInsertedId() instead)
    * BC Deleted Generator::getByCriteriaWithOrderAndLimit(), merged into Generator::getByCriteria() with $order and $limit parameters
    * BC Deleted UnitOfWork::generateUid() and UnitOfWork::generateUUID() methods
    * New feature: HydratorValueObject allows hydrating simple objects without metadata using native fetch_object() methods
    * New feature: ResultInterface::setObjectToFetch() for value object hydration (corrected entry: no fetchObject() method was ever exposed; setObjectToFetch() was removed in 4.0.0, see above)
    * Improvement: UnitOfWork now uses WeakMap exclusively for internal entity storage (enhanced from PHP 8.0 implementation)
    * Improvement: PHPStan analysis raised to level 7 with baseline significantly reduced (from ~2200 to <50 errors)
    * Improvement: Type coverage tool added (tomasvotruba/type-coverage) with minimum thresholds: 97% return types, 85% param types, 83% property types
    * Improvement: Rector configuration added for automated refactoring and code quality improvements (targeting PHP 8.0+)
    * Improvement: PHPStan custom extension for properties (CCMBenchmark\Ting\PHPStan\PropertiesExtension)

3.15.0 (2026-10-05):
    * Repository::get() (composite primary key), getBy() / getOneBy() criteria and the getBy() order now all take
      property names, converted to column names by the Metadata
    * Deprecate column names in these keys: a key that is not a property but is the column of a field still works
      and triggers a silenced E_USER_DEPRECATED. A key that is both a property and the column of another field is
      the property
    * Deprecate order directions other than ASC / DESC (case-insensitive): still ignored, they trigger a silenced
      E_USER_DEPRECATED and will throw a ValueException in 4.0. An order whose directions are all ignored no longer
      produces an invalid "ORDER BY" clause
    * Criteria values (getBy(), getOneBy() and get()) are converted by the serializer of their field:
        * an object is serialized (enum, DateTime, Uuid...); an object for a field without serializer throws a
          ValueException, except a Stringable object which is still sent as is
        * an array becomes an IN list of converted values, an empty array throws a ValueException
          (it produced an invalid "IN ()" before), check it first: if ($ids === []) { return ...; }
        * an array for a field whose serializer implements the new Serializer\ArrayValueInterface (Json does) is
          serialized as a whole and compared with "=". The result depends on the column type: a TEXT column matches
          when the encoding is the same, a MySQL JSON column compared to a string never matches, PostgreSQL has no
          "=" operator for json (jsonb has one)
        * null (IS NULL) and scalars are unchanged

3.14.0 (2026-10-05):
    * Security: prevent SQL injection in Pgsql Driver::getInsertedIdForSequence() (backport from 4.0.0-rc.1)
    * Drop Symfony 6 and PHP 8.1 support, PHP >= 8.2 is now required
    * Add Symfony 8 support
    * Tests: migrate from atoum to PHPUnit
    * Add primary/replica names: ConnectionPool::primary() / replica(), Connection::primary() / replica(),
      Query::selectPrimary() and Repository::pingPrimary()
    * Connection configuration now uses the "primary" and "replicas" keys
    * Deprecate ConnectionPool::master() / slave(), Connection::master() / slave(), Query::selectMaster()
      and Repository::pingMaster(): they trigger a silenced E_USER_DEPRECATED and delegate to the new methods
    * Deprecate the "master" and "slaves" connection configuration keys: they trigger a silenced E_USER_DEPRECATED
      and are converted to "primary" / "replicas" (the new key wins when both are given)
    * ConnectionPoolInterface::master() / slave() are deprecated, they will be replaced by primary() / replica() in 4.0.
      Connection uses primary() / replica() when the pool provides them, master() / slave() otherwise
    * Fix Cached\PreparedQuery: selectPrimary() (formerly selectMaster()) is now honoured for reading queries

3.13.0 (2026-09-24):
